60,3,1,talk,May,,"Option A is the only one that actually addresses the imbalance I found. Why should one person suffer while we just ""oversee"" it? Rotating the location is the only way to make this truly equal.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Mizuki,,"@May, equality in location doesn't guarantee a quality environment. If we rotate the point, we're just rotating the annoyance. Option B is more logical; it manages the actual cause of the complaints—the timing and presence of waste.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,Kenji,,"@Mizuki, @May, I'm worried about Option C! A permanent structure sounds great for smells, but it's really expensive. Even with the subsidy, the bill might be too high for some of us to pay at once.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Mizuki"",""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Midori,,"I'm worried that Option A might cause more friction, as moving the bins could lead to new arguments. Option B requires us to police each other, which I'm afraid might be too stressful for our relationships.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Servas,,"Option B asks us to oversee the point, which may lead to the same exhaustion we have seen before. Option A is a cycle of change, but Option C, while costly, offers a lasting peace.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,May,,"@Servas, ""lasting peace"" in Option C is just a fancy way of saying the bins stay at the same gate forever! That's not peace; that's just ignoring the person who's been burdened for years. Option A is the only fair choice.","{""words"":41,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Mizuki,,"@May, fairness isn't just about location; it's about the lived experience. Option C can trap odors and become unbearable in August, and Option A just shifts the problem. Option B addresses the root cause by limiting waste exposure.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Kenji,,"@Mizuki, but Option B doesn't stop the money leak! We're still spending thousands on nets and deodorants. If we can't afford Option C, maybe Option A is better because it doesn't cost extra money to move the bins?","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Mizuki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Midori,,"@Kenji, you're right that cost is a factor. But if we choose Option A, will we just be moving the tension from one house to another? I'm worried we'll just be rotating the resentment instead of solving it.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Servas,,"@Midori, your concern is a mirror of the truth. If we rotate the burden, we only rotate the fatigue. Option A is a carousel of duty, and Option B is a watchman's vigil. Let us seek the path of least exhaustion.","{""words"":41,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,May,,"@Servas, ""least exhaustion"" for us is ""maximum exhaustion"" for the person currently stuck with the bins! We can't keep pretending that's okay. Option A is the only one that stops one person from being the neighborhood's dumping ground.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Mizuki,,"@May, your focus is on the location, but the real issue is the waste itself. Option B's supervision is only about twenty mornings a year per house. It's a manageable burden that ensures the area stays clean for everyone.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,Kenji,,"@Mizuki, but who pays for the nets and deodorants in Option B? If we keep spending that money every year, it's like a hidden bill that never ends. Option A seems the cheapest and fairest for our wallets!","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Mizuki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Midori,,"@Kenji, I understand the cost is a worry, but I'm more concerned about our hearts. If we choose Option A, will we be okay with the bins moving to our own gates? I hope we choose the path that keeps us friends.","{""words"":42,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Servas,,"@May, your heart for the burdened is noble. Yet, we must ask: does moving the bins solve the labor, or merely the location? I fear Option B and A both demand a constant, draining vigilance from our spirits.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,May,,"@Servas, it's not just about labor; it's about respect. Keeping the bins at one gate is a permanent burden. Option A is the only choice that treats us as equals. I'm voting for A!","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,Mizuki,,"@May, respect is also about not creating a cycle of annoyance. Option C is a trap for odors, and A is just shifting the problem. Option B is the most sustainable and logical. I vote for B.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,Kenji,,I'm voting for A! It's the only one that doesn't cost a huge amount of money upfront or keep draining our wallets every year. It's fair for our budgets and fair for the space!,"{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,Midori,,"I'm torn, but I'll vote for Option A. While I worry about the tension, it seems the most fair to the person currently burdened, and I hope this shared effort helps us heal our relationships.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Servas,,"I see that the heart of this group leans toward equality of space. While I fear the fatigue of rotation, I value your unity more. I shall vote for Option A.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
